---
// « Mentions légales » (LCEN art. 6-III) — the ENGLISH twin, written by a human rather than
// translated. TEXT page: pure Astro, no island, zero JS — the only one on the site in that case,
// and the only one exercising ADR-0002's SSG rationale. Do not add interactivity here without
// reason: its value is being readable without running a line of script.
//
// The shell lives in `layouts/Page.astro` — which adds no script, so the zero-JS promise holds.
// The page’s own text stylesheet goes through the `page-style` slot; the LEGAL TEXT itself stays
// here rather than in `copy.*` (yuya's decision): it is a REFERENCE text, read as a block and not
// by table entries, and its English version is a TWIN page written by a human.
import { DEFAULT_LOCALE, type Locale, localePath } from '../../i18n/locales';
import Page from '../../layouts/Page.astro';
import { headCopy } from '../../ui/head-copy';

const locale = (Astro.currentLocale ?? DEFAULT_LOCALE) as Locale;
const head = headCopy(locale);
---

<Page title={head.legalTitle} description={head.legalDescription} path="/mentions-legales">
  {/* ⚠ THE MEASURE AND THE TYPE SCALE ARE THE SITE'S (Yul, 2026-08-05), and the gutter was the
      complaint: 20 px where every other page keeps 40, in a column capped at 680 while the rest of
      the site runs to 1080. Read one after the other, the legal page looked like a document from
      another product — narrower, tighter, and set two sizes smaller than anything around it.

      ⚠ THE PAGE IS STILL ZERO-JS, which is its whole value (see the header) and the reason it does
      NOT gain the site bar and the footer along with the measure: both are Preact islands, and one
      of them decides its layout from a hook that only runs once hydrated. A static copy of them
      here would be a second footer to keep in step with the first. So the shell is aligned and the
      chrome is not — knowingly, and it is the last thing that sets this page apart.

      The prose keeps a measure of its OWN (760 px), narrower than the page: a legal text set across
      a thousand pixels is one the eye loses the line of. That is the same split the rest of the site
      makes — a 1080 shell, text blocks that stop well before its edge. */}
  <style is:global slot="page-style">
    main {
      max-width: 1080px;
      margin: 0 auto;
      padding: 56px 40px 80px;
    }
    .back {
      display: inline-block;
      font-size: 13px;
      color: #8b98c0; /* NAVY.textMuted */
      text-decoration: none;
      margin-bottom: 36px;
    }
    .back:hover {
      color: #2fd4f0;
    }
    h1 {
      font-size: 38px;
      font-weight: 600;
      line-height: 1.15;
      letter-spacing: -0.025em;
      margin: 0 0 36px;
    }
    h2 {
      font-size: 20px;
      font-weight: 600;
      line-height: 1.25;
      letter-spacing: -0.015em;
      color: #dde4f4; /* NAVY.textHeading */
      margin: 44px 0 14px;
    }
    p,
    li {
      font-size: 15px;
      line-height: 1.7;
      color: #aab6d6; /* NAVY.textBody */
    }
    /* The prose's own measure — see the note above on why it is not the page's. */
    p,
    ul {
      max-width: 760px;
    }
    p {
      margin: 0 0 14px;
    }
    ul {
      margin: 0 0 14px;
      padding-left: 20px;
    }
    li {
      margin-bottom: 10px;
    }
    strong {
      color: #c8d2e8; /* NAVY.textSecondary */
    }
    .dim {
      font-size: 13px;
      color: #66739a; /* NAVY.textDim */
      margin-top: 40px;
    }
    /* The site's own threshold — the same 720 px `ui/v2/useIsMobile.ts` uses, and the same 480/20
       column the mobile mock-ups give every other page. */
    @media (max-width: 720px) {
      main {
        max-width: 480px;
        padding: 36px 20px 56px;
      }
      h1 {
        font-size: 27px;
      }
      h2 {
        font-size: 18px;
        margin: 34px 0 12px;
      }
    }
  </style>
    <main>
      <a class="back" href={localePath(locale, '/')}>← Back</a>

      <h1>Legal notice</h1>

      <h2>Publisher</h2>
      <p>
        PanoptiCool is published on a non-professional, non-profit basis by <strong>Yuya</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Contact: <a href="mailto:yuya@panopti.cool">yuya@panopti.cool</a>
      </p>

      <h2>Host</h2>
      <p>
        OVH SAS — 2 rue Kellermann, 59100 Roubaix, France<br />
        Phone: 1007 · RCS Lille Métropole 424 761 419<br />
        <a href="https://www.ovhcloud.com/fr/">ovhcloud.com</a>
      </p>

      <h2>Personal data</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>PanoptiCool collects nothing.</strong> This isn't a promise, it's a consequence of
        how it's built.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Your export never leaves your device.</strong> It is not sent, uploaded or stored:
          the site is a set of static files, and the analysis runs in your browser. There is no
          server to send it to.
        </li>
        <li><strong>No cookies</strong>, no trackers, no analytics.</li>
        <li><strong>No account</strong>, no forms, no sign-up.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>No connection logs.</strong> The server that serves you this page keeps neither
          your IP address nor your visit.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        There is therefore no processing of personal data within the meaning of the GDPR. There is
        no right of access, rectification or erasure to exercise: there is nothing to consult,
        correct or delete.
      </p>

      <h2>The AI analysis, if you use it</h2>
      <p>
        Both analyses offer to have part of your export read by a language model: your comments and
        searches on the TikTok side, <strong>the content of one conversation</strong> you pick
        yourself on the Instagram side. That model runs <strong>on your own machine</strong>, in a
        server you start yourself. Nothing is sent to a third party, and this analysis only begins
        when you explicitly click.
      </p>

      <h2>The content of your messages</h2>
      <p>
        The Instagram analysis does not read your conversations in order to display them all: it
        counts their volumes and their rhythms. The <strong>text</strong> of a thread is read only if
        you open that thread, or if you send it to the local model — in both cases on your gesture,
        in memory, and without being kept. Closing the tab is enough for nothing to remain: there is
        no server, no database, no file written.
      </p>

      <h2>What PanoptiCool displays</h2>
      <p>
        The tool shows what an algorithm <em>could</em> infer from a data export. It is not a
        diagnosis, nor a verdict on a person: it is a demonstration of a system. The inferences
        shown can be wrong, and when they are, that is also the point.
      </p>

      <h2>Code and licence</h2>
      <p>
        The code is free software, under the <a href="https://www.gnu.org/licenses/agpl-3.0.html"
          >AGPL-3.0-only</a
        > licence. You can read it, check it and reuse it:
        <a href="https://github.com/lagayayuya/PanoptiCool">github.com/lagayayuya/PanoptiCool</a>.
      </p>

      <p class="dim">
        This page is an English translation provided for convenience. The French version (<a
          href={localePath('fr', '/mentions-legales')}>mentions légales</a
        >) is the legally binding text.
      </p>
    </main>
</Page>
